WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.314
Welcome to the story deck
for Building Azure Security,

2
00:00:03.314 --> 00:00:07.013
a developer workbook in the
Federal Modernization Workbook.

3
00:00:07.013 --> 00:00:09.427
Instead of nine chapters one at a time,

4
00:00:09.427 --> 00:00:14.099
we'll follow one application all the
way through: Contoso's public portal,

5
00:00:14.099 --> 00:00:16.980
its subscription, its
Front Door and gateway,

6
00:00:16.980 --> 00:00:20.251
its network, its key vault,
and the hub that serves it.

7
00:00:20.251 --> 00:00:24.371
It's fictional: every value comes
from the kit's own test fixtures,

8
00:00:24.371 --> 00:00:28.025
and every output on a slide is
what the kit really printed.

9
00:00:28.025 --> 00:00:29.600
Two notes before we begin.

10
00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:32.538
The chapters are drafts
awaiting the owner's review.

11
00:00:32.538 --> 00:00:36.014
And the code is illustrative,
tested against fixtures,

12
00:00:36.014 --> 00:00:38.460
not against a live subscription.

13
00:00:42.210 --> 00:00:43.198
Here's the route.

14
00:00:43.198 --> 00:00:46.136
Scene one reads the
landing-zone baseline back.

15
00:00:46.136 --> 00:00:50.223
Scene two lists every public face
of the portal, with its owner.

16
00:00:50.223 --> 00:00:55.384
Scene three asks which of its public IP
addresses are really under DDoS protection.

17
00:00:55.384 --> 00:00:59.848
Scene four finds a web application
firewall that logs but doesn't block.

18
00:00:59.848 --> 00:01:04.198
Scene five checks whether the portal's
origins can be reached around the edge.

19
00:01:04.198 --> 00:01:06.236
Scene six follows its egress.

20
00:01:06.236 --> 00:01:08.423
Scene seven reads its key vault.

21
00:01:08.423 --> 00:01:13.610
Scene eight holds it all with policy,
compares two weeks, and records the evidence.

22
00:01:13.610 --> 00:01:16.722
And scene nine takes the
kit live, test first.

23
00:01:16.722 --> 00:01:18.574
Each scene has a story slide,

24
00:01:18.574 --> 00:01:22.560
a try-it slide with real output,
and a one-question check.

25
00:01:26.310 --> 00:01:27.135
Scene one.

26
00:01:27.135 --> 00:01:32.263
The guide's Step 1 sets four things once:
the hierarchy settings, the initiatives

27
00:01:32.263 --> 00:01:37.235
in audit at the intermediate root, platform
logs, and Defender for Cloud plans.

28
00:01:37.235 --> 00:01:41.757
Microsoft Learn gives the commands,
and its REST reference gives the reads,

29
00:01:41.757 --> 00:01:45.622
but nobody runs them back every
week across every subscription.

30
00:01:45.622 --> 00:01:49.448
So the kit does, with one check
scoped to the intermediate root.

31
00:01:49.448 --> 00:01:51.623
For our portal, two things turn up.

32
00:01:51.623 --> 00:01:54.786
Its subscription's Key Vault
plan is on the Free tier.

33
00:01:54.786 --> 00:01:57.951
And the hub firewall's public
address does send logs,

34
00:01:57.951 --> 00:02:01.485
but to an application team's
workspace, which doesn't count:

35
00:02:01.485 --> 00:02:05.900
a log counts only if it's enabled
and goes to the platform workspace.

36
00:02:09.650 --> 00:02:10.375
Try it.

37
00:02:10.375 --> 00:02:14.675
From the kit's folder, run the baseline
check on the first week's estate.

38
00:02:14.675 --> 00:02:18.531
It reads the hierarchy settings,
four policy assignments,

39
00:02:18.531 --> 00:02:24.075
sixteen platform resources and four
subscriptions, from six calls, every one a read.

40
00:02:24.075 --> 00:02:26.687
Twelve findings and one information row.

41
00:02:26.687 --> 00:02:30.955
Read them: creating management
groups doesn't need authorization;

42
00:02:30.955 --> 00:02:35.456
the firewall and its public IP send
nothing to the platform workspace;

43
00:02:35.456 --> 00:02:39.724
the online subscription, the portal's,
has its Key Vault plan on Free;

44
00:02:39.724 --> 00:02:44.200
and the FedRAMP initiative is in
audit, exactly as Step 1 intends.

45
00:02:47.950 --> 00:02:48.762
Check one.

46
00:02:48.762 --> 00:02:52.261
The hub firewall's public
address has a diagnostic setting

47
00:02:52.261 --> 00:02:55.688
that sends all its logs to an
application team's workspace.

48
00:02:55.688 --> 00:02:57.512
Does the kit count that as logging?

49
00:02:57.512 --> 00:02:58.850
Any enabled setting?

50
00:02:58.850 --> 00:03:01.013
Only one to the platform workspace?

51
00:03:01.013 --> 00:03:03.938
Yes, if the application
team owns the address?

52
00:03:03.938 --> 00:03:06.238
Or only when Defender for Cloud is on?

53
00:03:06.238 --> 00:03:08.650
The answer opens the next scene.

54
00:03:12.400 --> 00:03:14.263
The answer to check one: no.

55
00:03:14.263 --> 00:03:17.701
Only an enabled log to the
platform workspace counts.

56
00:03:17.701 --> 00:03:18.563
Scene two.

57
00:03:18.563 --> 00:03:23.291
Steps 3 to 5 can't be proved complete
without a list of what's exposed,

58
00:03:23.291 --> 00:03:27.503
and the portal is public in many
places: a Front Door profile,

59
00:03:27.503 --> 00:03:32.488
the gateway's address, a jump box, a
load balancer, two web apps and a vault.

60
00:03:32.488 --> 00:03:37.216
The kit joins the guide's Resource
Graph queries, built from Microsoft

61
00:03:37.216 --> 00:03:41.700
Learn's starter sample, into one
register, with an owner on every row.

62
00:03:41.700 --> 00:03:44.187
The jump box's address has no owner.

63
00:03:44.187 --> 00:03:48.975
A week later a new address appears,
with no owner either, and an orphan

64
00:03:48.975 --> 00:03:54.720
address is gone, with a reminder to remove
any DNS record still pointing at it.

65
00:03:58.470 --> 00:03:59.195
Try it.

66
00:03:59.195 --> 00:04:04.220
Build the first week's register: nineteen
exposures, sixteen with an owner.

67
00:04:04.220 --> 00:04:05.708
Read the portal's rows.

68
00:04:05.708 --> 00:04:08.956
The gateway's public address
carries the gateway's

69
00:04:08.956 --> 00:04:11.895
web application firewall
policy, wafp-portal.

70
00:04:11.895 --> 00:04:14.308
The Front Door profile carries its own.

71
00:04:14.308 --> 00:04:16.794
The jump box's address has no owner.

72
00:04:16.794 --> 00:04:20.458
Then build the register again a
week later, against the first one.

73
00:04:20.458 --> 00:04:25.494
The new address, pip-new-api, is
reported as new, and the orphan as gone,

74
00:04:25.494 --> 00:04:31.820
with the reminder to remove any DNS record
that still points at its old address.

75
00:04:35.570 --> 00:04:36.395
Check two.

76
00:04:36.395 --> 00:04:39.122
The register lists every public address,

77
00:04:39.122 --> 00:04:42.170
Front Door profile and
gateway, with its owner.

78
00:04:42.170 --> 00:04:43.470
Where should it be kept?

79
00:04:43.470 --> 00:04:46.682
In a public repository,
so assessors can find it?

80
00:04:46.682 --> 00:04:48.582
Inside the agency's boundary?

81
00:04:48.582 --> 00:04:50.557
Only in the portal's own subscription?

82
00:04:50.557 --> 00:04:53.840
Or nowhere, since it's
regenerated each week?

83
00:04:57.590 --> 00:05:01.140
The answer to check two:
inside the agency's boundary,

84
00:05:01.140 --> 00:05:05.515
never in a public repository,
because it maps the attack surface.

85
00:05:05.515 --> 00:05:06.565
Scene three.

86
00:05:06.565 --> 00:05:11.069
Microsoft Learn's Zero Trust
recommendations check that every public

87
00:05:11.069 --> 00:05:15.115
address in a virtual network is
protected, with metrics and logs.

88
00:05:15.115 --> 00:05:19.865
The jump box's address says it inherits
its protection, which sounds protected.

89
00:05:19.865 --> 00:05:23.213
But the kit follows it to its
network, the portal's spoke,

90
00:05:23.213 --> 00:05:25.965
and that network has no
DDoS protection plan.

91
00:05:25.965 --> 00:05:27.340
So it's unprotected.

92
00:05:27.340 --> 00:05:31.490
The gateway's address inherits from
the hub, which is linked to the plan.

93
00:05:31.490 --> 00:05:34.616
And it's the one address
with both DDoS logs to the

94
00:05:34.616 --> 00:05:38.950
platform workspace and an enabled
alert on Under DDoS attack.

95
00:05:42.700 --> 00:05:43.425
Try it.

96
00:05:43.425 --> 00:05:45.787
Read each public address's status.

97
00:05:45.787 --> 00:05:48.926
The hub's three addresses
inherit from the hub network.

98
00:05:48.926 --> 00:05:53.325
The test application's address has
IP Protection on the address itself.

99
00:05:53.325 --> 00:05:56.518
The jump box's inherits
from the portal's spoke,

100
00:05:56.518 --> 00:05:59.038
which has no plan, so it's unprotected.

101
00:05:59.038 --> 00:06:03.269
The load balancer's is unknown:
its protection depends on a backend

102
00:06:03.269 --> 00:06:07.962
network the frontend doesn't name, and
the kit says so rather than guessing.

103
00:06:07.962 --> 00:06:10.797
Then the findings: the
jump box unprotected,

104
00:06:10.797 --> 00:06:14.971
the firewall's address with no logs
to the workspace and no alert,

105
00:06:14.971 --> 00:06:19.360
and the test application's alert,
which exists but is disabled.

106
00:06:23.110 --> 00:06:24.022
Check three.

107
00:06:24.022 --> 00:06:28.373
The jump box's address has the protection
mode virtual network inherited.

108
00:06:28.373 --> 00:06:30.560
What decides whether
it's actually protected?

109
00:06:30.560 --> 00:06:33.723
Nothing, because inherited
always means protected?

110
00:06:33.723 --> 00:06:36.985
Whether its network is linked
to a DDoS protection plan?

111
00:06:36.985 --> 00:06:38.773
Whether it has an owner tag?

112
00:06:38.773 --> 00:06:41.410
Or whether Front Door sits in front of it?

113
00:06:45.160 --> 00:06:48.468
The answer to check three:
whether its network is linked

114
00:06:48.468 --> 00:06:51.560
to a DDoS protection plan,
and the portal's spoke isn't.

115
00:06:51.560 --> 00:06:52.535
Scene four.

116
00:06:52.535 --> 00:06:55.821
DDoS protection stops at
layers three and four;

117
00:06:55.821 --> 00:06:59.022
layer seven needs a web
application firewall.

118
00:06:59.022 --> 00:07:01.168
Microsoft Learn is plain about it:

119
00:07:01.168 --> 00:07:05.384
detection mode logs and forwards,
and only prevention mode blocks.

120
00:07:05.384 --> 00:07:08.597
The portal's gateway policy
is still in detection.

121
00:07:08.597 --> 00:07:12.723
Its Front Door policy blocks, but
has no bot protection rule set.

122
00:07:12.723 --> 00:07:16.466
The kit checks every policy on
both services the same way,

123
00:07:16.466 --> 00:07:20.686
and writes the switch to prevention
to a plan, which it never runs.

124
00:07:20.686 --> 00:07:25.230
The application owner runs it,
after reviewing the false positives.

125
00:07:28.980 --> 00:07:29.705
Try it.

126
00:07:29.705 --> 00:07:31.143
Read each policy.

127
00:07:31.143 --> 00:07:34.808
The gateway's policy,
wafp-portal: detection,

128
00:07:34.808 --> 00:07:39.492
body inspection on, rule set
2.1, bot rules and a rate limit.

129
00:07:39.492 --> 00:07:46.167
The Front Door policy: prevention, body
inspection on, rule set 2.1, no bot rules.

130
00:07:46.167 --> 00:07:50.320
Then read the plan the kit wrote: a
header that says the kit did not run it,

131
00:07:50.320 --> 00:07:54.830
then Microsoft Learn's own switch command
for wafp-portal, with its resource

132
00:07:54.830 --> 00:07:59.268
group and subscription, setting
prevention, the state and body inspection.

133
00:07:59.268 --> 00:08:04.680
One of three internet-facing endpoints is
behind a firewall in prevention this week.

134
00:08:08.430 --> 00:08:09.342
Check four.

135
00:08:09.342 --> 00:08:11.631
The gateway's policy is in detection,

136
00:08:11.631 --> 00:08:15.293
and the kit wrote the switch to
prevention into a plan file.

137
00:08:15.293 --> 00:08:16.617
Who runs it, and when?

138
00:08:16.617 --> 00:08:18.818
The kit, on its next weekly run?

139
00:08:18.818 --> 00:08:23.406
The application owner, after the
false-positive review, in a change window?

140
00:08:23.406 --> 00:08:25.405
Azure Policy, automatically?

141
00:08:25.405 --> 00:08:28.360
Or nobody, because detection is enough?

142
00:08:32.110 --> 00:08:35.089
The answer to check four:
the application owner,

143
00:08:35.089 --> 00:08:38.298
after the false-positive
review, in a change window.

144
00:08:38.298 --> 00:08:39.322
Scene five.

145
00:08:39.322 --> 00:08:44.036
A web application firewall only helps if
nothing can reach the origin around it.

146
00:08:44.036 --> 00:08:48.762
Microsoft Learn says to use the Front Door
service tag and the Front Door ID header

147
00:08:48.762 --> 00:08:53.210
together, because the tag's addresses
are shared by every Front Door customer.

148
00:08:53.210 --> 00:08:57.710
The portal's east web app does
both, with this profile's own ID.

149
00:08:57.710 --> 00:09:00.322
The west web app allows the tag alone.

150
00:09:00.322 --> 00:09:02.822
So any customer's Front Door can reach it.

151
00:09:02.822 --> 00:09:05.893
The kit reads each origin's
access restrictions

152
00:09:05.893 --> 00:09:08.734
and compares the header
with the profile's ID.

153
00:09:08.734 --> 00:09:11.488
And it knows configuration isn't proof:

154
00:09:11.488 --> 00:09:14.940
the guide's test is a
direct request, refused.

155
00:09:18.690 --> 00:09:19.415
Try it.

156
00:09:19.415 --> 00:09:21.477
Read each origin's lock-down.

157
00:09:21.477 --> 00:09:25.334
In the portal's origin group, the
east web app is locked with the

158
00:09:25.334 --> 00:09:29.115
service tag and the Front Door
ID; the west one has the tag only.

159
00:09:29.115 --> 00:09:34.267
In the API group, one origin reaches its
back end over Private Link and one is

160
00:09:34.267 --> 00:09:39.252
a public host the kit can't check, so it
says to test a direct request by hand.

161
00:09:39.252 --> 00:09:42.945
Then the findings: the west
origin's rule has no header,

162
00:09:42.945 --> 00:09:48.680
and the API group mixes a private and a
public origin, which Learn says not to do.

163
00:09:52.430 --> 00:09:53.368
Check five.

164
00:09:53.368 --> 00:09:58.393
The west origin allows the Front Door
service tag, with no Front Door ID header.

165
00:09:58.393 --> 00:09:59.568
What's wrong with that?

166
00:09:59.568 --> 00:10:02.243
Nothing, since the tag
is Front Door's own?

167
00:10:02.243 --> 00:10:05.431
The tag's addresses are shared
by every Front Door customer?

168
00:10:05.431 --> 00:10:07.742
The tag blocks Front Door's health probes?

169
00:10:07.742 --> 00:10:10.460
Or the tag works only with Private Link?

170
00:10:14.210 --> 00:10:17.531
The answer to check five: the
tag's addresses are shared by

171
00:10:17.531 --> 00:10:21.135
every Front Door customer, so the
header has to prove it's yours.

172
00:10:21.135 --> 00:10:22.823
Scene six, what leaves.

173
00:10:22.823 --> 00:10:27.834
Microsoft Learn's Zero Trust recommendations
for Azure Firewall check that outbound

174
00:10:27.834 --> 00:10:29.991
traffic routes through the firewall,

175
00:10:29.991 --> 00:10:33.959
with threat intelligence and
intrusion detection in alert and deny.

176
00:10:33.959 --> 00:10:38.247
The portal's web subnet sends its
default route straight to the internet.

177
00:10:38.247 --> 00:10:42.284
Its jump subnet sends it to an
appliance that isn't the hub firewall.

178
00:10:42.284 --> 00:10:44.610
And the hub policy is still in alert.

179
00:10:44.610 --> 00:10:49.446
The kit writes Learn's draft-and-deploy
commands for the move to deny into a plan,

180
00:10:49.446 --> 00:10:54.350
which it never runs, because deploying
replaces the whole applied policy.

181
00:10:58.100 --> 00:10:58.825
Try it.

182
00:10:58.825 --> 00:11:01.575
Read each workload subnet's default route.

183
00:11:01.575 --> 00:11:05.838
Two go to the hub firewall at 10.0.0.4.

184
00:11:05.838 --> 00:11:08.912
The payroll data subnet
has no route table at all.

185
00:11:08.912 --> 00:11:12.254
The portal's jump subnet goes
to an unknown appliance,

186
00:11:12.254 --> 00:11:14.988
and its web subnet
straight to the internet.

187
00:11:14.988 --> 00:11:17.126
Two of five route to the firewall.

188
00:11:17.126 --> 00:11:21.287
Then read the plan: Microsoft Learn's
four commands, create a draft,

189
00:11:21.287 --> 00:11:26.298
set threat intelligence and intrusion
detection to deny, show it, and deploy it,

190
00:11:26.298 --> 00:11:29.844
each with the policy's resource
group and subscription,

191
00:11:29.844 --> 00:11:33.200
under a header that says
the kit did not run them.

192
00:11:36.950 --> 00:11:37.975
Check six.

193
00:11:37.975 --> 00:11:40.446
Why does the kit write
the move to deny as a

194
00:11:40.446 --> 00:11:43.063
draft-and-deploy plan,
instead of sending it?

195
00:11:43.063 --> 00:11:44.624
Because drafts are cheaper?

196
00:11:44.624 --> 00:11:47.753
Because deploying replaces
the whole applied policy,

197
00:11:47.753 --> 00:11:50.325
so it goes through review
and a change window?

198
00:11:50.325 --> 00:11:53.000
Because deny can't be set
from the command line?

199
00:11:53.000 --> 00:11:55.870
Or because the kit has no network access?

200
00:11:59.620 --> 00:12:03.827
The answer to check six: deploying
replaces the whole applied policy,

201
00:12:03.827 --> 00:12:06.558
so it goes through review
and a change window.

202
00:12:06.558 --> 00:12:08.269
Scene seven, the vault.

203
00:12:08.269 --> 00:12:13.309
Microsoft Learn's Key Vault recommendations
are clear: Azure role-based access,

204
00:12:13.309 --> 00:12:16.745
not access policies; soft
delete and purge protection;

205
00:12:16.745 --> 00:12:20.945
private endpoints with public
access disabled; and audit logging.

206
00:12:20.945 --> 00:12:23.220
The portal's vault misses every part.

207
00:12:23.220 --> 00:12:28.550
It uses access policies, has no purge
protection, a firewall that allows all

208
00:12:28.550 --> 00:12:31.903
networks, public access
on, no private endpoint,

209
00:12:31.903 --> 00:12:34.482
and no audit events to the workspace.

210
00:12:34.482 --> 00:12:37.458
The payroll team's vault
next door has every part,

211
00:12:37.458 --> 00:12:40.790
so the estate already
shows what done looks like.

212
00:12:44.540 --> 00:12:45.265
Try it.

213
00:12:45.265 --> 00:12:46.665
Read each vault.

214
00:12:46.665 --> 00:12:51.186
The hub's certificate vault has
role-based access, purge protection,

215
00:12:51.186 --> 00:12:56.353
a closed firewall and audit logs, but
public access on and no private endpoint.

216
00:12:56.353 --> 00:12:58.103
The payroll vault has everything.

217
00:12:58.103 --> 00:13:03.414
The portal's vault has nothing: no
role-based access, no purge protection,

218
00:13:03.414 --> 00:13:08.816
a firewall that allows all networks,
public access, no endpoints, no audit.

219
00:13:08.816 --> 00:13:11.423
Then its findings, one per missing part,

220
00:13:11.423 --> 00:13:14.765
from access policies to
the missing audit events.

221
00:13:14.765 --> 00:13:17.530
One of three vaults has the full posture.

222
00:13:21.280 --> 00:13:22.218
Check seven.

223
00:13:22.218 --> 00:13:25.943
The fix for the portal's vault
includes turning on purge protection.

224
00:13:25.943 --> 00:13:27.568
Can that be rolled back later?

225
00:13:27.568 --> 00:13:29.668
Yes, with a key vault update?

226
00:13:29.668 --> 00:13:33.606
No, because once it's on it can't be
turned off, and that's the point?

227
00:13:33.606 --> 00:13:35.355
Only by Microsoft support?

228
00:13:35.355 --> 00:13:37.680
Or only in a test subscription?

229
00:13:41.430 --> 00:13:43.480
The answer to check seven: no.

230
00:13:43.480 --> 00:13:47.780
Once on, purge protection can't be
turned off, and that's the point.

231
00:13:47.780 --> 00:13:48.643
Scene eight.

232
00:13:48.643 --> 00:13:53.552
The portal's WAF tuning runs under a
waiver that expires on December first,

233
00:13:53.552 --> 00:13:56.368
so the kit warns it's
due within thirty days.

234
00:13:56.368 --> 00:14:01.063
A week later, the platform team switches
the gateway's firewall to prevention,

235
00:14:01.063 --> 00:14:05.099
adds the west origin's header, routes
the web subnet to the firewall,

236
00:14:05.099 --> 00:14:07.593
and turns on the vault's purge protection.

237
00:14:07.593 --> 00:14:11.404
A new address arrives, with
no owner and no protection.

238
00:14:11.404 --> 00:14:14.229
The kit compares the two
weeks by fingerprint,

239
00:14:14.229 --> 00:14:17.944
and records each run as evidence
that supports the controls,

240
00:14:17.944 --> 00:14:22.530
with its key security indicators,
its hashes and the record before it.

241
00:14:26.280 --> 00:14:27.005
Try it.

242
00:14:27.005 --> 00:14:30.992
The policy check flags the portal's
waiver, due December first.

243
00:14:30.992 --> 00:14:34.305
Compare the two weeks:
thirteen fixed, four new.

244
00:14:34.305 --> 00:14:38.140
Among the fixed, the portal's
firewall policy, the west origin,

245
00:14:38.140 --> 00:14:41.592
the web subnet's route and
the vault's purge protection.

246
00:14:41.592 --> 00:14:46.531
Among the new, the arriving address,
with no owner and no protection plan.

247
00:14:46.531 --> 00:14:50.419
Record the run as evidence: it
covers all nine area steps,

248
00:14:50.419 --> 00:14:54.730
and the check shows the hashes
match and the chain is unbroken.

249
00:14:54.730 --> 00:14:58.393
Then ask whether this evidence could
promote anything to production.

250
00:14:58.393 --> 00:15:02.220
Every stage step fails:
no passing live record.

251
00:15:05.970 --> 00:15:06.757
Check eight.

252
00:15:06.757 --> 00:15:10.958
The evidence folder's hashes
match, and its chain is unbroken.

253
00:15:10.958 --> 00:15:14.032
Why does the test-stage
check still fail every step?

254
00:15:14.032 --> 00:15:15.408
Is the chain too short?

255
00:15:15.408 --> 00:15:20.258
Do the records come from fixture runs, when
only live records count toward a stage?

256
00:15:20.258 --> 00:15:22.633
Are the key security indicators wrong?

257
00:15:22.633 --> 00:15:24.770
Or were the metrics not recorded?

258
00:15:28.520 --> 00:15:32.226
The answer to check eight: the
records come from fixture runs,

259
00:15:32.226 --> 00:15:34.820
and only live records
count toward a stage.

260
00:15:34.820 --> 00:15:36.733
Scene nine, the runbook.

261
00:15:36.733 --> 00:15:40.811
It's one ordered set of commands,
run against a test subscription,

262
00:15:40.811 --> 00:15:44.120
then production, and only
the settings file changes.

263
00:15:44.120 --> 00:15:46.295
The weekly run needs only Reader.

264
00:15:46.295 --> 00:15:51.116
The kit's one write is the security initiative's
assignment at the intermediate root

265
00:15:51.116 --> 00:15:54.146
in DoNotEnforce, which
evaluates without enforcing,

266
00:15:54.146 --> 00:15:57.520
as Microsoft Learn's assignment
structure page describes.

267
00:15:57.520 --> 00:16:00.454
It's a plan, applied only
with its approved hash,

268
00:16:00.454 --> 00:16:04.082
and its rollback is rehearsed
in test before production.

269
00:16:04.082 --> 00:16:07.245
Go or no-go is the test
stage's live evidence.

270
00:16:07.245 --> 00:16:12.310
One honest label: nobody has run this
runbook against a live subscription.

271
00:16:16.060 --> 00:16:16.785
Try it.

272
00:16:16.785 --> 00:16:19.685
The runbook's own
rehearsal on the fixtures.

273
00:16:19.685 --> 00:16:24.633
The first weekly run reads every
area, records itself as the baseline,

274
00:16:24.633 --> 00:16:27.672
and covers every area
step and the metrics.

275
00:16:27.672 --> 00:16:31.536
A week later, the second run
finds last week's, compares them,

276
00:16:31.536 --> 00:16:35.722
thirteen fixed and four new, and
its record adds the comparison.

277
00:16:35.722 --> 00:16:38.896
Then the rollback for
production, back to audit:

278
00:16:38.896 --> 00:16:42.722
one command, printed with its
plan's hash, and nothing sent.

279
00:16:42.722 --> 00:16:47.800
In a live stage, a second person approves
that hash before anyone applies it.

280
00:16:51.550 --> 00:16:53.400
Check nine, the last one.

281
00:16:53.400 --> 00:16:56.300
An operator runs production's
preflight before

282
00:16:56.300 --> 00:16:59.200
the test stage's evidence
passes its stage check.

283
00:16:59.200 --> 00:17:03.322
Does it pass anyway, fail on the
check that reads the test evidence,

284
00:17:03.322 --> 00:17:08.120
warn and let the operator decide, or
assign the initiative regardless?

285
00:17:11.870 --> 00:17:15.331
The answer to check nine: the
preflight's last check fails,

286
00:17:15.331 --> 00:17:19.308
because production needs the test
stage's evidence to pass first.

287
00:17:19.308 --> 00:17:24.145
That's the portal, end to end: its baseline
read back, every public face owned,

288
00:17:24.145 --> 00:17:27.238
its protection traced to
the network behind it,

289
00:17:27.238 --> 00:17:32.630
a web application firewall switched by a
person, origins locked to the edge, egress

290
00:17:32.630 --> 00:17:37.944
through the firewall, its vault fixed, and
every week's drift recorded as evidence.

291
00:17:37.944 --> 00:17:39.082
Now it's your turn.

292
00:17:39.082 --> 00:17:43.725
Each chapter has a second lab in your
own test subscription, never production,

293
00:17:43.725 --> 00:17:47.570
with Reader and the guide's own
Validate commands beside the kit.

294
00:17:47.570 --> 00:17:51.348
Then go deeper: the draft chapters
and their narrated tours,

295
00:17:51.348 --> 00:17:54.432
the learning path, and
the implementation guide.

296
00:17:54.432 --> 00:17:55.600
Thanks for watching.
